WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.850
JEFF DAVIS: Welcome to the
Google Cloud Platform Education

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:03.780
series.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.150
My name is Jeff Davis,
and in this session,

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.490
we'll discuss adding
machine learning

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.770
into your applications.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:14.140
We'll be running a moderated
Q&amp;A during the session,

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.635
so feel free to ask
questions using the Ask

00:00:16.635 --> 00:00:19.840
Your Question field at
the bottom of your screen.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.710
Let's get started.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:24.520
We'll begin by doing
a series of demos.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:30.190
We'll show the capabilities of
Google's machine learning APIs,

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:33.100
and we'll show how you can
access those through simple web

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.530
interfaces or
through incorporating

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:39.580
them using client libraries
in your own applications.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.380
Then we'll move on
and talk about machine

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:44.170
learning as a whole on
the Google Cloud platform.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:46.840
We'll talk about use of the
APIs and their capabilities

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:47.380
and pricing.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:51.580
We'll also talk about Google
Cloud Machine Learning

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:55.360
Engine, which is the general
purpose platform on which you

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.550
build, train, deploy, and
query your own machine learning

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:01.390
models.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:03.250
We'll finish up by
talking about how

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:05.830
to get more information
after this session,

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:08.380
how to evaluate the
opportunities that you have

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:11.350
for using Google's
machine learning features

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:13.240
in your own products,
and again, we'll

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:17.460
be doing Q&amp;A
throughout the session.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:20.840
So let's start with some demos.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:22.890
We're going to
switch over to here,

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:26.640
and I'm going to show you
the capabilities of each

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.120
of the machine learning vision--

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:32.070
or each of the machine learning
APIs through the simple web

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:34.830
interface that you
can find online.

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:37.340
So we'll start with
the Vision API.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:41.580
The Vision API allows you
to very easily identify

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:45.880
characteristics and content
information about pictures,

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.220
so let's take a look at that.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.150
You'll notice, here,
I can select a photo.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:57.480
I'm going to pick a photo
off of my hard drive,

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:03.150
and we'll drag this
into the web browser.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:07.170
Now, this is making a machine
learning API call to the Vision

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:11.400
API, and you can see the
whole range of characteristics

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.090
that this API can give
you information about.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:16.860
So it's analyzing
the person's face,

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.660
and it's trying to
identify is the person

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.180
happy, sad, angry, surprised.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:24.644
You'll notice there's not a
whole lot of positives here.

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:27.060
We basically have found he
doesn't seem to be super happy,

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.790
doesn't seem to be angry,
surprised, or whatever.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.340
If we look at labels, we
can see that it's then

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:36.990
a pretty good job of
identifying appropriate labels

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.970
for the photo, so we can
see that it's a person.

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:43.100
We can see that it's
probably a surgeon.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:48.030
Not sure where arm comes in, but
physician sounds pretty good.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.370
You can use this,
therefore, to actually go

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.980
through a range of
images, identify

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:55.335
the content of the images,
and take appropriate actions.

00:02:55.335 --> 00:02:57.570
I'll show you an
application of some

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:03.430
of these things in my own demo
applications in just a sec.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:04.480
You can get properties.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:07.030
So for instance, this
tells you what colors

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:08.470
are used in the photo.

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:11.110
So if you had an
application, for instance,

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:14.050
that was going to scan
the internet for pictures

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:18.160
of your product, and you wanted
pictures that had certain color

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:21.520
schemes because those would look
good in your current marketing

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:25.890
campaign's layouts, you
could easily do that.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:29.040
Safe Search basically
identifies whether or not

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.210
the content might
be inappropriate,

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:32.910
so is it adult content?

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:34.560
Is it violent?

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:35.970
Is it spoof content?

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:39.780
This is content where it's
sort of sarcastic in nature

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:43.330
and might not be appropriate
for your applications.

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:47.280
And then the JSON here,
this is the actual response

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.890
of all of the
characteristics that

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:53.010
get returned to you
by the Vision API.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:55.500
So what does this all have
to do with machine learning?

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.810
Well, what Google
is doing is they

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:01.350
are taking tons and
tons and tons of images

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:03.780
and corresponding
labeling information,

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:07.110
and they're training
a model specifically

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:11.970
at identifying the information
about faces, labels,

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.460
properties,
inappropriate content,

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:16.209
and so forth on images.

00:04:16.209 --> 00:04:19.740
So this is a very
purposeful API,

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:22.560
but it's one with wide
and common applications.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.170
Lots of applications
could make use of this,

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:28.290
and so they make it available.

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:30.920
If we look at the
Speech API, again, you

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:32.810
have a similar demonstration.

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:37.520
Notice the Speech API can work
in a variety of languages.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.920
I'm going to use
English, and I'm just

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:43.640
going to speak to
the web browser.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:48.050
We need to plan a
Q1 business review,

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:51.950
and our top priority
for Q2 is a proof

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:54.930
of concept on the Professional
Edition and an upgrade.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:01.550
You can see that it
has actually taken--

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:04.590
it's transcribed
what I had to say,

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:07.450
and this would be super
helpful, for instance,

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:10.590
if you incorporated this into
applications that your customer

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:13.170
success managers or
your sales rep use.

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:15.930
It's hard enough to get
those folks to enter

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:18.960
good data into your CRM system.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:19.990
This makes it easier.

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:22.290
They can just talk
into an application,

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:26.580
have it transcribe, and
automatically add it.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:29.730
Let's look at the
Cloud Translation API.

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:36.240
So I'm going to switch this
from English to Chinese.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.614
I can't type
Chinese, so it's not

00:05:38.614 --> 00:05:40.530
going to make a lot of
sense, and I'm actually

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:43.730
going to choose to
translate this into Spanish.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:59.570
So then if I say, we need to
schedule a business review,

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:02.900
you'll notice that it
translates it on the fly,

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:06.800
although I should
spell things correctly.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:18.960
And the primary goal for Q2 is
to perform a proof of concept

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:24.900
on Pro Edition and
arrange an up-sell.

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:31.740
Now, you'll notice that there
are two different translations

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:32.250
available.

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:35.530
There's the Standard Edition
and the Premium Edition.

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:40.710
The Premium Edition is intended
for larger blocks of text,

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:43.980
and rather than doing sort of
a word by word or short phrase

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:46.200
by a short phrase
translation, it's

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.870
looking at the context
of the overall document,

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:52.320
and is able to use
large quantities of text

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:56.100
and the context in order to
make more intelligent decisions

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:59.280
about its translation.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:04.500
The fourth API that I'll show
you is the cloud language--

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:08.700
Cloud Natural Language
API, and what this does

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:12.810
is it allows you to enter text,
and it will analyze the text

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:17.860
and sort of draw three
different sets of insights.

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:20.130
So you'll notice,
the first is what's

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:21.630
called entity extraction.

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:24.720
So when it looks
at the text, it's

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:29.990
able to identify Google
is an organization,

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:36.090
Android is a consumer
good, Sundar is a person,

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:39.600
the Consumer Electronics
Show is an event,

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:43.950
so it's able to actually go
through and identify entities

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:47.460
and then link to
information on the internet

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:49.020
about those entities.

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:58.520
On the other hand, if I said,
I really liked Google Hangouts

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:01.700
better than anything else.

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:07.530
It was clearly as good
as Zoom, for instance.

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:09.380
You'll notice it's
able to extract

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:12.510
that Zoom is an
organization, Google Hangouts

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:14.860
is a consumer good--

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:18.040
anything is sort of
interesting, so it's

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:20.410
not perfect, but pretty good.

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:22.780
Now, in addition to being
able to extract entities,

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:24.980
which is really
useful, for instance,

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:27.970
if you want to scan a bunch
of tweets about your product

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:29.890
and find out what
other products are

00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:32.470
mentioned in the same tweets.

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:34.750
That way, you get a better
idea of what your customers

00:08:34.750 --> 00:08:37.350
consideration sets are.

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:41.140
You might also want to know how
they feel about your product,

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:43.980
so we take the same
sentence, and we

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:49.350
have the Natural Language
API do sentiment analysis.

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:52.800
And you can see that the
overall sentiment is positive,

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:55.030
so this goes from
negative 1 for really,

00:08:55.030 --> 00:08:58.530
really negative to positive 1
for really, really positive.

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:04.050
So this is a 0.4, which is
good, and this number gives you

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:05.850
a strength of the feeling.

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:07.815
So it's really good,
but you can be sort of,

00:09:07.815 --> 00:09:09.898
eh, it's really good, but
it's not that important.

00:09:09.898 --> 00:09:12.270
Or it could be really good,
and it's really important.

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:19.110
So you'll notice that the first
sentence was a 0.4 and a 0.4,

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:23.230
and the second sentence was
actually slightly better.

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:31.600
Now, if I said it was
almost as good as Zoom,

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:34.930
and we analyze
that, you'll notice

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:36.940
continues to be really strong.

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:40.480
If you say, I hated this.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:48.550
My video was grainy,
and my session

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:53.080
kept getting
disconnected probably

00:09:53.080 --> 00:10:00.530
because I was hijacking
my neighbor's wifi--

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:04.130
not that anybody would
actually do that.

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:05.710
Again, if we look
at the sentiment,

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:08.590
you'll notice overall
sentiment is negative,

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:12.460
and in fact, the word hate
clearly denotes really

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:17.170
strong dissatisfaction, also
fairly strong overall magnitude

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:18.680
of sentiment.

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:22.220
The third category of analysis
that the Natural Language

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:27.440
API performs for you is
a syntactical analysis.

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:29.870
So this actually
does a bunch of stuff

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:32.910
that's a little
beyond my expertise,

00:10:32.910 --> 00:10:35.610
but it's able to
sort of indicate

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:38.400
how the various words are
related to one another

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.850
and what parts of speech
each one is, and so forth.

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:46.260
So this could be useful, but
the first two are the ones

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:49.940
that I've seen more typically
used within applications

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:52.650
by the customers
that I work with.

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:56.360
So obviously, the
functionality is really cool.

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:58.980
It's great and all,
but you can't really

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:03.450
do business by going to each of
these special purpose machine

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:07.410
learning APIs and working
interactively with the UIs.

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:08.970
So what you really
want to do is you

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:12.750
want to have your developers
be able to consume

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:17.735
these insights from the machine
learning APIs within programs.

00:11:17.735 --> 00:11:19.110
So what we're
going to do here is

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:24.570
I'm going to shrink this down,
and we'll shrink this down,

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:28.660
and I've got a couple
of examples here.

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:33.280
So you'll notice I've got
some code in my Finder window.

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:40.430
I'm going to actually look at
that code in my code editor

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:44.660
here, and so the first one that
I'm using is the Speech API.

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:46.510
So the Speech API--

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:49.790
I'm using an example that
Google has provided us,

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:55.970
and I'm using the Google
Cloud Python client libraries.

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:58.850
And so what I'm going to
do here is, in Quick Start,

00:11:58.850 --> 00:12:03.470
you can see that I'm
going to import speech

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:05.480
from Google Cloud library.

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:08.270
I'm going to create
a speech client.

00:12:08.270 --> 00:12:10.310
I'm going to open
a file, and this

00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:13.700
is a recorded file that's
very much like the content

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:16.550
that I spoke into the web page.

00:12:16.550 --> 00:12:22.490
And I'm going to open the file,
read it in as an audio sample,

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:25.820
and then I'm going to ask the
speech client to actually do

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:30.730
asynchronous recognition,
extract the content out

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:34.400
of the audio file,
and transcribe that.

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:35.605
So let's see if that works.

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:40.950
That's not the one we want.

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:44.110
That's-- let's see if
this is what we want.

00:12:49.690 --> 00:12:54.660
That's the image API, so we'll
come back to that in a second.

00:12:54.660 --> 00:13:07.520
So this-- let's run it,
and if all goes well,

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:10.240
we should get
output that contains

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:14.116
the transcript of the audio
file that I recorded earlier.

00:13:14.116 --> 00:13:15.490
And so you'll see,
the transcript

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:19.290
says we need to schedule
and delivering--

00:13:19.290 --> 00:13:21.200
I didn't speak as
clearly as I might of--

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:22.480
a Q1 business review.

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:26.020
Primary goal in Q2 is
to do a proof of concept

00:13:26.020 --> 00:13:28.870
and close the opportunity
for the pro upgrade.

00:13:28.870 --> 00:13:30.099
OK, not bad.

00:13:30.099 --> 00:13:32.140
Now, again, I'm doing this
from the command line.

00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:34.810
I haven't incorporated
it into a full app yet,

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:39.340
but this shows you
how straightforward

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:41.770
it is to actually incorporate.

00:13:41.770 --> 00:13:46.810
So if we go to the Vision API,
we have a similar example.

00:13:46.810 --> 00:13:49.210
So the Quick Start
here basically says

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:52.450
let's import the vision library
instead of the speech library.

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:54.670
Let's create a vision client.

00:13:54.670 --> 00:13:58.990
Let's find the wakeupcat
file, and let's

00:13:58.990 --> 00:14:01.510
feed it to the
Vision API and see

00:14:01.510 --> 00:14:03.190
what information we get back.

00:14:03.190 --> 00:14:05.350
And specifically, I want
to look at the labels.

00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:07.150
I don't need to see
all the other stuff.

00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:09.440
I just want to see the labels.

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:15.040
So let's go back and I'm going
to close the speech client,

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:18.282
and let's actually
open the wakeupcat

00:14:18.282 --> 00:14:19.990
so you can see what
the image looks like.

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:26.530
There's our cat, and
we're going to feed

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:29.960
this image to the Vision API
and see what it has to say.

00:14:34.180 --> 00:14:37.510
So takes a moment or two
to run, and it comes back,

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:39.520
and it gives you the labels.

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:41.230
This is similar to
the set of labels

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:45.610
that were displayed in the web
UI during the first example

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:46.120
that I gave.

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:50.620
So this identifies the picture
as having a cat, the cat

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:56.080
being a mammal, the cat
having whiskers, the cat being

00:14:56.080 --> 00:14:59.170
a small to medium sized
cat as opposed to a big cat

00:14:59.170 --> 00:15:02.180
like a lion or a tiger.

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:05.150
This could be a cat
like mammal, so that

00:15:05.150 --> 00:15:07.280
would be like a
links or something

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:11.090
like that, something that isn't
a cat but looks like a cat.

00:15:11.090 --> 00:15:14.720
And then animal shelter
is the last one,

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:16.550
and I'm not sure which
components made it

00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:18.260
look like the animal shelter.

00:15:18.260 --> 00:15:22.910
Perhaps the industrial blinds
in the background, but probably

00:15:22.910 --> 00:15:28.350
lower level of confidence
in that prediction.

00:15:28.350 --> 00:15:31.740
OK, these are great, again,
little code examples.

00:15:31.740 --> 00:15:34.530
But, Jeff, I want to
see something meatier.

00:15:34.530 --> 00:15:37.140
I want to see something
actually live and running

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:39.690
and show all of Google's
machine learning

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:43.080
prowess being brought to bear
in a real business problem.

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:46.890
OK, so let's take a look
at a live application.

00:15:46.890 --> 00:15:50.790
This application is a
customer success application.

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:54.540
It's used by a post-sales
customer facing team

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:59.340
to monitor customer's
engagement and experiences

00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:02.820
with the product, and
to intervene proactively

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:06.240
to help drive better outcomes
for the customer and better

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:08.520
up-sell and renewal for
the product, and so forth.

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:12.090
And the product at hand is a
video conferencing product.

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:12.955
People do calls.

00:16:12.955 --> 00:16:15.330
When they're done with the
call, they can leave a rating,

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:18.920
but they can also
leave a text message,

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:21.390
a certain chunk of feedback.

00:16:21.390 --> 00:16:23.760
And we want to be able
to analyze whether or not

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:25.470
that feedback is
positive or negative,

00:16:25.470 --> 00:16:28.980
and we want to be able to deal
with feedback in a variety

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:30.510
of different languages.

00:16:30.510 --> 00:16:36.650
So what I'm going to do is
go to the Comments page,

00:16:36.650 --> 00:16:40.130
and what we have here
is a proof of concept.

00:16:40.130 --> 00:16:42.770
We have a list of comments.

00:16:42.770 --> 00:16:45.530
These comments are provided
in different languages.

00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:47.840
So you notice the first
comments in English,

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:53.180
the second comment is in
French, the third comment

00:16:53.180 --> 00:16:57.650
is in Spanish, the fourth
comment is in Chinese,

00:16:57.650 --> 00:17:00.770
and the fifth comment
is in Swahili.

00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:04.260
So clearly, we have customers
all around the world.

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:09.140
Now, if I were an English-only
customer success manager,

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:12.050
and I was just looking at
the raw feedback provided

00:17:12.050 --> 00:17:16.640
by customers, I would have no
idea which 4/5 of my comments

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:17.910
meant.

00:17:17.910 --> 00:17:21.630
But notice here, I am
getting the original comment,

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:24.030
and then I'm also getting
an English translation,

00:17:24.030 --> 00:17:28.200
so this is saying it was good,
the audio was a bit squeaky.

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:31.170
This was saying, I was
disappointed that there's

00:17:31.170 --> 00:17:33.520
no support for Linux.

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:36.640
Obviously, happy camper
with this comment.

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:39.090
Another happy camper here.

00:17:39.090 --> 00:17:42.690
Additionally, not only
does this translate

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:47.130
from the original
language to English,

00:17:47.130 --> 00:17:50.100
it also performs a sentiment
analysis and color codes

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:52.410
that so I can
easily take a look.

00:17:52.410 --> 00:17:54.300
I can see, for instance,
that this clearly

00:17:54.300 --> 00:17:58.680
seems to be a strongly
happy customer.

00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:01.260
This customer is all right.

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:04.980
This customer is not at
all happy, and so forth.

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:09.390
Now, again, this is
just a proof of concept.

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:12.480
It would be less useful to
have an individual looking

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:14.460
at this interactively
than to be performing,

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:17.370
say, a mass analysis, where
you take all of the comments

00:18:17.370 --> 00:18:20.790
from all of your users
over the last 30 days,

00:18:20.790 --> 00:18:24.690
and you aggregate them,
looking at sentiment analysis,

00:18:24.690 --> 00:18:28.500
and that you identify what
percentage of comments

00:18:28.500 --> 00:18:33.540
are strongly negative versus
sort of medium versus strongly

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:34.470
positive.

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:36.720
And you can perform
outreach to customers

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:40.050
that are strongly positive in
order to cultivate champions,

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:43.440
whereas you could reach
out to unhappy customers

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:46.800
to try to do account recovery or
solve whatever problem they're

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:48.270
running into.

00:18:48.270 --> 00:18:52.320
Implementing this was just as
easy as the quick little demos

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:54.270
that I gave you previously.

00:18:54.270 --> 00:18:58.050
So if I go in, and
I look here, you

00:18:58.050 --> 00:19:01.900
can see this is where I
put the sample comments.

00:19:01.900 --> 00:19:06.370
And up at the top, I've
imported two client libraries,

00:19:06.370 --> 00:19:10.780
so I've imported the translate
library from the Google Cloud

00:19:10.780 --> 00:19:14.140
client, and I've imported
the language library.

00:19:14.140 --> 00:19:19.110
And then I make a translate
client and a language client,

00:19:19.110 --> 00:19:20.650
and then to do the
translation, you

00:19:20.650 --> 00:19:23.720
can see the first thing that
I do is detect the language.

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:26.950
So I don't know ahead of time
what language the comment is

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:30.670
in, and I have to tell the
Translation API what language

00:19:30.670 --> 00:19:33.140
to translate from and to.

00:19:33.140 --> 00:19:35.740
So the first thing that
I do is I detect that,

00:19:35.740 --> 00:19:38.320
and then I actually
do the translation,

00:19:38.320 --> 00:19:40.450
and then I'm going to
output the translation.

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:46.390
Similarly, I used the
natural language client

00:19:46.390 --> 00:19:50.950
to create a document from
the text that was provided.

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:54.280
Now, you'll notice I'm using the
translated the English content.

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:55.780
For some languages,
you can actually

00:19:55.780 --> 00:19:57.910
do this with the
native language.

00:19:57.910 --> 00:20:00.700
French, for instance--
native detection

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:03.830
with the natural language
API is not available,

00:20:03.830 --> 00:20:06.190
so I translated
everything into English.

00:20:06.190 --> 00:20:08.530
And then I do a
sentiment analysis,

00:20:08.530 --> 00:20:11.639
and I'm going to use the
sentiment score to output.

00:20:11.639 --> 00:20:13.930
Now, obviously, there isn't
any sort of error handling.

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:15.970
You're going to want to-- if
you were doing production code,

00:20:15.970 --> 00:20:17.350
it would be more robust.

00:20:17.350 --> 00:20:20.830
But this gives you a sense
that, in 10 or 15 lines of code,

00:20:20.830 --> 00:20:26.020
I am now tapping into this
highly trained, ever evolving

00:20:26.020 --> 00:20:29.080
API for language
and for translation,

00:20:29.080 --> 00:20:33.430
and I get the benefits directly
in my application with very

00:20:33.430 --> 00:20:34.960
little real development time.

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:38.470
This legitimately took me from--

00:20:38.470 --> 00:20:42.010
the first time I did this,
this took me less than an hour

00:20:42.010 --> 00:20:45.820
to read the documentation,
figure out the APIs,

00:20:45.820 --> 00:20:49.680
write the code, and
start consuming.

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:52.260
So four different APIs.

00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:55.260
We have Vision,
Speech, Translation,

00:20:55.260 --> 00:20:57.810
and Natural Language,
and these all

00:20:57.810 --> 00:21:01.960
can be very easily incorporated
into your applications

00:21:01.960 --> 00:21:05.570
tomorrow with a
small amount of code.

00:21:05.570 --> 00:21:07.520
So really, you just
have to think about what

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:09.770
use cases you can leverage.

00:21:09.770 --> 00:21:14.720
Now, let's say that
you need something more

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:16.530
specific to your environment.

00:21:16.530 --> 00:21:19.820
So Vision is nice,
Speech is nice,

00:21:19.820 --> 00:21:25.220
but let's say you want
to do damage assessment.

00:21:25.220 --> 00:21:29.120
You want to look
at images of roofs,

00:21:29.120 --> 00:21:30.960
and based on the
damage to the roof,

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:33.170
you want to identify
what the cost is

00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:35.060
going to be to repair that.

00:21:35.060 --> 00:21:37.190
This is being done by
insurance companies now.

00:21:37.190 --> 00:21:39.440
They actually will fly drones.

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:42.860
The drone will go up, fly
over, take video footage,

00:21:42.860 --> 00:21:46.370
that footage gets uploaded
to a machine learning model.

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:48.950
The machine learning
model can then actually

00:21:48.950 --> 00:21:52.130
do estimates on how long
it will take to repair

00:21:52.130 --> 00:21:53.840
and how much it
will cost to repair.

00:21:53.840 --> 00:21:55.400
In order to do
that, obviously, you

00:21:55.400 --> 00:21:57.920
can't use one of these
very general purpose

00:21:57.920 --> 00:21:59.990
machine learning APIs.

00:21:59.990 --> 00:22:02.540
You would have to
build your own model.

00:22:02.540 --> 00:22:06.560
I'm not going to demo that
because, if you actually look--

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:09.765
let's do Cloud ML Engine Demo.

00:22:13.170 --> 00:22:14.530
We've got some samples here.

00:22:18.770 --> 00:22:22.080
If we did the introductory
example for Cloud ML Engine--

00:22:22.080 --> 00:22:26.690
so this is building-- taking
a dataset from the census

00:22:26.690 --> 00:22:29.390
and building a model
that will predict,

00:22:29.390 --> 00:22:35.720
based on census demographics,
what your earnings will be.

00:22:35.720 --> 00:22:37.880
I just want to show you
real quick what's entailed

00:22:37.880 --> 00:22:40.820
in setting up this model.

00:22:40.820 --> 00:22:44.540
So you'll notice this article
goes on for a little while.

00:22:44.540 --> 00:22:49.660
It's not actually terribly
hard to make this intro work,

00:22:49.660 --> 00:22:54.250
but it would take significantly
longer than we have today

00:22:54.250 --> 00:22:56.920
to get everything built,
to train the models,

00:22:56.920 --> 00:23:00.040
to test them, and then
get them up to speed.

00:23:00.040 --> 00:23:03.490
A big part of this,
beyond what you

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:06.760
would see in this introduction,
is that you actually

00:23:06.760 --> 00:23:08.590
have to collect the right data.

00:23:08.590 --> 00:23:12.340
You have to sanitize the data,
You have to format the data.

00:23:12.340 --> 00:23:17.320
The quality and quantity of
your data is really critical.

00:23:17.320 --> 00:23:19.210
If I were to demo it,
what I would probably

00:23:19.210 --> 00:23:22.900
do is do another demo here,
and what you would want to do

00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:25.910
is actually predict churn.

00:23:25.910 --> 00:23:28.630
So what customer success teams
are really, really interested

00:23:28.630 --> 00:23:31.210
in is looking at these
renewals and finding

00:23:31.210 --> 00:23:33.220
who's coming up for
renewals that you might not

00:23:33.220 --> 00:23:35.410
get a full renewal on.

00:23:35.410 --> 00:23:38.050
And that's kind of the holy
grail of customer success,

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:41.170
is being able to identify
potential churn candidates

00:23:41.170 --> 00:23:43.332
and then intervene
in enough time

00:23:43.332 --> 00:23:44.540
that you can make it go away.

00:23:44.540 --> 00:23:49.190
Clearly, Google
doesn't have a model

00:23:49.190 --> 00:23:52.940
already created and serving for
my particular customer base.

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:54.590
I would have to create my own.

00:23:54.590 --> 00:23:58.070
I would also need a
relatively good set of data

00:23:58.070 --> 00:24:01.370
from a relatively significant
number of customers.

00:24:01.370 --> 00:24:04.580
So since this is a demo, I
don't have all of that at hand,

00:24:04.580 --> 00:24:06.060
but I think you get the point.

00:24:06.060 --> 00:24:07.940
So hopefully, this
has really begun

00:24:07.940 --> 00:24:10.640
sort of getting you
thinking about how you could

00:24:10.640 --> 00:24:15.080
use either the canned
APIs or your own models

00:24:15.080 --> 00:24:17.850
in your applications.

00:24:17.850 --> 00:24:21.260
So lets talk a little bit more
about the general capabilities

00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:25.460
of machine learning across
Google Cloud Platform.

00:24:25.460 --> 00:24:27.860
So in the bottom
row here, you have

00:24:27.860 --> 00:24:30.350
the machine learning
offerings, and the four

00:24:30.350 --> 00:24:34.280
items on the right, you
should be quite familiar with.

00:24:34.280 --> 00:24:36.200
There's the Vision
API, the Speech API,

00:24:36.200 --> 00:24:39.140
the Natural Language API, and
the Cloud Translation API.

00:24:39.140 --> 00:24:42.710
Google also just did
a Video Insights API,

00:24:42.710 --> 00:24:45.530
where you can
actually now extract

00:24:45.530 --> 00:24:48.020
information similar to what
you get out of the Vision

00:24:48.020 --> 00:24:49.190
API for still images.

00:24:49.190 --> 00:24:51.830
You can actually do
this out of videos now.

00:24:51.830 --> 00:24:56.390
And then on the left is the
Cloud Machine Learning Engine,

00:24:56.390 --> 00:24:58.520
so the Google Cloud
Machine Learning Engine,

00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:01.040
which is quite a mouthful,
but its also quite

00:25:01.040 --> 00:25:03.020
a handful of technology.

00:25:03.020 --> 00:25:07.880
Basically it uses the TensorFlow
model development framework,

00:25:07.880 --> 00:25:11.570
and it automatically provisions
and builds the clusters

00:25:11.570 --> 00:25:14.000
necessary to train your model.

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:16.760
And then you can deploy your
model and run predictions

00:25:16.760 --> 00:25:18.620
against the model.

00:25:18.620 --> 00:25:24.950
The top row here is a bunch
of tools used in big data

00:25:24.950 --> 00:25:26.900
and analytics workloads.

00:25:26.900 --> 00:25:30.800
And remember, I said that the
care and feeding of your data

00:25:30.800 --> 00:25:33.320
that you feed into your
machine learning models

00:25:33.320 --> 00:25:35.090
is absolutely critical.

00:25:35.090 --> 00:25:37.340
And so you're going to want
to leverage big query data

00:25:37.340 --> 00:25:42.890
flow, data proc, data lab in
order to collect your data,

00:25:42.890 --> 00:25:45.200
organize your data,
sanitize your data,

00:25:45.200 --> 00:25:49.160
and get it ready to feed into
the Cloud Machine Learning

00:25:49.160 --> 00:25:50.990
Engine.

00:25:50.990 --> 00:25:54.211
So a few details about the APIs
and about the machine learning

00:25:54.211 --> 00:25:54.710
engine.

00:25:54.710 --> 00:25:58.920
So the Cloud Vision
API is accessible

00:25:58.920 --> 00:26:01.160
through a simple REST API.

00:26:01.160 --> 00:26:06.450
It can detect labels,
logos, landmarks, and faces,

00:26:06.450 --> 00:26:10.620
so an example that we might
use in my customer success

00:26:10.620 --> 00:26:15.420
application is I might
want to actually find

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:20.970
all of the photos of my
product in certain verticals.

00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:24.570
And so what I want to do
is I want to find our logo,

00:26:24.570 --> 00:26:26.370
but I then also
want to get labels

00:26:26.370 --> 00:26:29.280
that indicate whether it's
an industrial environment

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:33.450
or a medical environment
or a social environment.

00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:36.180
And that way, I can actually
extract those photos

00:26:36.180 --> 00:26:41.100
and put them in collateral
aimed at those verticals.

00:26:41.100 --> 00:26:44.820
It can do OCR, so you can
obviously extract the text

00:26:44.820 --> 00:26:47.530
content out of the images.

00:26:47.530 --> 00:26:50.880
If you run any sort of a
platform where customers

00:26:50.880 --> 00:26:55.590
are creating and sharing
content with other customers,

00:26:55.590 --> 00:26:58.110
you clearly are
concerned about what

00:26:58.110 --> 00:27:01.440
gets posted because you don't
want inappropriate content--

00:27:01.440 --> 00:27:04.800
this is either violent content
or things like nudity--

00:27:04.800 --> 00:27:07.560
to poison the well
in your community.

00:27:07.560 --> 00:27:10.470
So you can actually
run all of your stuff

00:27:10.470 --> 00:27:14.760
through the Google Cloud Vision
API before it gets submitted,

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:17.640
and you can use this to
take appropriate action

00:27:17.640 --> 00:27:20.024
if inappropriate
content happens.

00:27:20.024 --> 00:27:21.690
And obviously, all
the image attributes,

00:27:21.690 --> 00:27:24.090
so again, this is if you
want to find all of the files

00:27:24.090 --> 00:27:26.820
in your giant photo
repository that

00:27:26.820 --> 00:27:32.230
meet certain conditions in terms
of color scheme, for instance.

00:27:32.230 --> 00:27:37.890
The pricing for making use of
the Vision API is $1.50 per

00:27:37.890 --> 00:27:40.680
1,000 units, and you get an
initial allotment of units

00:27:40.680 --> 00:27:43.480
for free.

00:27:43.480 --> 00:27:47.440
The second API that we talked
about was the Speech API.

00:27:47.440 --> 00:27:50.580
The Speech API
supports recognition

00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:53.370
of up to 80 plus languages.

00:27:53.370 --> 00:27:54.870
It can be real
time are recorded,

00:27:54.870 --> 00:27:57.570
so we showed you
real time when I

00:27:57.570 --> 00:28:00.180
was speaking into
the website, and you

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:02.490
can build that
functionality fairly easily.

00:28:02.490 --> 00:28:04.710
Google actually makes
a quiet library.

00:28:04.710 --> 00:28:08.910
It's optimized to detect
the active speaker,

00:28:08.910 --> 00:28:11.220
even when there is
surrounding noise.

00:28:11.220 --> 00:28:13.770
This is really important
because, very often,

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:16.920
when somebody's speaking, it's
not in an enclosed studio,

00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:19.560
but rather they may
be out on the street,

00:28:19.560 --> 00:28:22.560
they may be in an open
floor plan office,

00:28:22.560 --> 00:28:24.450
and you want to
be able to extract

00:28:24.450 --> 00:28:27.030
the speech from
the primary speaker

00:28:27.030 --> 00:28:31.850
and ignore the background noise
and background conversations.

00:28:31.850 --> 00:28:34.250
If you are in an
environment where

00:28:34.250 --> 00:28:37.460
you have a lot of
technical jargon or words

00:28:37.460 --> 00:28:41.700
that would not be common
in everyday speech,

00:28:41.700 --> 00:28:48.380
you can actually provide a word
or phrase hints to the API.

00:28:48.380 --> 00:28:53.990
So you can say, for instance,
that gas and energy terminology

00:28:53.990 --> 00:28:56.870
that isn't in
everyday conversation

00:28:56.870 --> 00:28:59.870
might be common in the audio
inputs that you're providing,

00:28:59.870 --> 00:29:02.540
and so you provide a list
of those phrases and words,

00:29:02.540 --> 00:29:05.540
and it's more likely that
they will be recognized.

00:29:05.540 --> 00:29:07.610
And like the Vision
API, it can actually

00:29:07.610 --> 00:29:10.700
identify inappropriate
content and filter that out.

00:29:10.700 --> 00:29:14.150
Pricing for the Google
Cloud Speech API

00:29:14.150 --> 00:29:17.690
is 6/10 of a cent
per 15 seconds,

00:29:17.690 --> 00:29:22.450
with the first 60
minutes being free.

00:29:22.450 --> 00:29:27.350
The Google Cloud Translation
API can work with a hundred

00:29:27.350 --> 00:29:29.270
different languages.

00:29:29.270 --> 00:29:31.910
As I noted in the example that
I gave in the customer success

00:29:31.910 --> 00:29:36.300
application, it can do
automatic language detection.

00:29:36.300 --> 00:29:40.890
The model is being consistently
upgraded by Google,

00:29:40.890 --> 00:29:44.080
so it's constantly
getting better.

00:29:44.080 --> 00:29:45.932
You start with a
maximum quota in terms

00:29:45.932 --> 00:29:47.890
of the number of characters
that you can spend,

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:49.600
but you can easily
adjust this up

00:29:49.600 --> 00:29:53.609
if you do large
amounts of translation.

00:29:53.609 --> 00:29:55.400
And as I noted, there
is a Premium Edition,

00:29:55.400 --> 00:29:59.020
and the Premium Edition is
less for translating phrases

00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:02.600
in short quantities of text,
and rather for translating

00:30:02.600 --> 00:30:04.640
large blocks of text.

00:30:04.640 --> 00:30:07.340
And it is able to use the
context throughout the block

00:30:07.340 --> 00:30:11.570
to do sort of better
narrative translation.

00:30:11.570 --> 00:30:15.690
Pricing here starts at $20 per
million characters translated.

00:30:18.480 --> 00:30:23.430
The Google Cloud Natural
Language API, as we saw,

00:30:23.430 --> 00:30:26.640
can do three types of
insight extraction.

00:30:26.640 --> 00:30:29.730
It can pull out the
entities and identify

00:30:29.730 --> 00:30:31.470
who those entities are.

00:30:31.470 --> 00:30:34.470
It can do sentiment analysis,
which is the most common use

00:30:34.470 --> 00:30:36.210
that I've seen in the field.

00:30:36.210 --> 00:30:40.080
And it can do
syntactical analysis.

00:30:40.080 --> 00:30:41.450
It supports multiple languages.

00:30:41.450 --> 00:30:45.800
Not all languages of origination
are supported, so for instance,

00:30:45.800 --> 00:30:47.570
French isn't.

00:30:47.570 --> 00:30:49.520
But you can try it
in your own language,

00:30:49.520 --> 00:30:52.880
and if you don't-- if it doesn't
work in the default language

00:30:52.880 --> 00:30:55.130
that you would prefer, you
can actually translate it

00:30:55.130 --> 00:30:56.510
to English and do that.

00:30:56.510 --> 00:30:59.570
Pricing starts at $1
per million units,

00:30:59.570 --> 00:31:01.790
with the first 5,000
units being free.

00:31:04.900 --> 00:31:08.650
Now these again are
Google's canned models.

00:31:08.650 --> 00:31:09.910
They're heavily trained.

00:31:09.910 --> 00:31:15.310
They're really, really strong,
but they are general purpose.

00:31:15.310 --> 00:31:19.850
They don't necessarily solve the
problem that you are tackling,

00:31:19.850 --> 00:31:21.590
so you can build
your own models.

00:31:21.590 --> 00:31:23.630
Now, the way that Google
has built their models

00:31:23.630 --> 00:31:27.210
is they have fed large
quantities of data,

00:31:27.210 --> 00:31:31.370
so an example here would
be training the Vision API.

00:31:31.370 --> 00:31:35.600
You feed it a bunch of images,
and with each of these images,

00:31:35.600 --> 00:31:38.830
you provide an
appropriate label.

00:31:38.830 --> 00:31:44.410
You run a very large,
well-crafted dataset

00:31:44.410 --> 00:31:48.850
through a mathematical
model, and once you've

00:31:48.850 --> 00:31:51.970
trained it, what's happening is
the machine learning process,

00:31:51.970 --> 00:31:54.010
in this case,
running an ML Engine,

00:31:54.010 --> 00:31:57.760
it's going to continue to
tweak this mathematical model

00:31:57.760 --> 00:32:00.490
until it has a very
high probability

00:32:00.490 --> 00:32:04.420
that if you put in a cat,
what's going to come out

00:32:04.420 --> 00:32:06.850
is the label cat.

00:32:06.850 --> 00:32:09.340
Now, obviously, you
probably don't want

00:32:09.340 --> 00:32:11.410
to replicate the Vision API.

00:32:11.410 --> 00:32:15.410
You can just pay
Google by the image,

00:32:15.410 --> 00:32:18.490
but what I would do here,
for say the customer success

00:32:18.490 --> 00:32:21.910
application, is
instead of images,

00:32:21.910 --> 00:32:25.180
I would give descriptions
of customers.

00:32:25.180 --> 00:32:28.510
So for every customer
of our product,

00:32:28.510 --> 00:32:30.880
when did they buy, how
big a company they are,

00:32:30.880 --> 00:32:33.400
how much did they buy
initially, how much have

00:32:33.400 --> 00:32:37.330
they used the product, is usage
going up, is usage going down.

00:32:37.330 --> 00:32:40.270
And I would give
as many features

00:32:40.270 --> 00:32:45.070
as I think are likely
relevant, and then I

00:32:45.070 --> 00:32:49.060
would indicate whether or
not these customers renewed

00:32:49.060 --> 00:32:53.050
or scaled back their
purchases or up-sold.

00:32:53.050 --> 00:32:54.970
And the goal is
to create a model

00:32:54.970 --> 00:32:57.880
that, instead of
spitting out cat, instead

00:32:57.880 --> 00:33:00.910
when I give it a new customer
with a bunch of features

00:33:00.910 --> 00:33:04.810
describing their interactions
with our company and product,

00:33:04.810 --> 00:33:07.270
they give me out a
label that indicates

00:33:07.270 --> 00:33:13.970
high probability of renewal
or high probability of churn.

00:33:13.970 --> 00:33:15.590
To do this, you would--

00:33:15.590 --> 00:33:18.980
again, you would use Cloud
Machine Learning Engine.

00:33:18.980 --> 00:33:22.280
You define your
models in TensorFlow,

00:33:22.280 --> 00:33:26.390
which is a machine learning
model development framework.

00:33:26.390 --> 00:33:29.030
This is a portable framework,
so you can develop your models

00:33:29.030 --> 00:33:31.520
and run them on Cloud
Machine Learning Engine,

00:33:31.520 --> 00:33:33.260
but you can also take
them and run them

00:33:33.260 --> 00:33:36.860
on whatever other
infrastructure you want.

00:33:36.860 --> 00:33:39.080
The Machine Learning Engine
is tightly integrated

00:33:39.080 --> 00:33:41.060
with other Google
services, so it's

00:33:41.060 --> 00:33:45.110
easy to pull in data from Google
Cloud Storage, for instance.

00:33:45.110 --> 00:33:48.680
Or you could do a bunch of
your analysis in BigQuery

00:33:48.680 --> 00:33:53.510
and dump the results out into
your Machine Learning Engine

00:33:53.510 --> 00:33:55.280
project.

00:33:55.280 --> 00:33:57.920
They have a feature
called HyperTuning.

00:33:57.920 --> 00:34:00.380
So hyper parameters
are parameters

00:34:00.380 --> 00:34:04.970
that are not feature
related on individual items,

00:34:04.970 --> 00:34:08.480
but rather how deep do you
want the neural network to be.

00:34:08.480 --> 00:34:10.840
How many leaves do you want?

00:34:10.840 --> 00:34:14.719
HyperTune will automatically
adjust those parameters for you

00:34:14.719 --> 00:34:17.960
and find what choices are going
to give you the highest level

00:34:17.960 --> 00:34:21.380
of accuracy in your models.

00:34:21.380 --> 00:34:22.909
It's quite scalable.

00:34:22.909 --> 00:34:27.320
So you can go ahead-- if you
do that demo that I showed you,

00:34:27.320 --> 00:34:29.015
the example that
Google gives you,

00:34:29.015 --> 00:34:33.260
you start by doing training
with a single instance.

00:34:33.260 --> 00:34:36.380
But you can scale that up
and do distributed training.

00:34:36.380 --> 00:34:39.510
You can train your model
using a single instance.

00:34:39.510 --> 00:34:41.510
You can train your model
using 10 instances.

00:34:41.510 --> 00:34:45.020
You can train your model
using 75 instances, and so on.

00:34:45.020 --> 00:34:48.980
And so this allows you to
feed more data into your model

00:34:48.980 --> 00:34:51.020
training faster.

00:34:51.020 --> 00:34:53.330
I mentioned that the
models are portable,

00:34:53.330 --> 00:34:58.100
and again, the whole training
and deployment and use

00:34:58.100 --> 00:35:00.830
of your model is a
fully managed service.

00:35:00.830 --> 00:35:03.984
You don't have to worry
about acquiring hardware.

00:35:03.984 --> 00:35:05.900
You don't even have to
worry about spinning up

00:35:05.900 --> 00:35:06.980
individual VMs.

00:35:06.980 --> 00:35:10.640
You simply turn the dial,
say you want to train,

00:35:10.640 --> 00:35:13.430
and Google will
automatically scale out

00:35:13.430 --> 00:35:15.860
the resources as necessary.

00:35:15.860 --> 00:35:21.590
Pricing for ML Engine starts
at $0.49 per node hour

00:35:21.590 --> 00:35:25.760
for training and $0.40 per
node hour for prediction,

00:35:25.760 --> 00:35:31.010
plus there's an additional $0.10
per 1,000 predictions charge.

00:35:31.010 --> 00:35:34.460
Again, if you want to
have an idea of the cost

00:35:34.460 --> 00:35:36.680
that your project may
incur ahead of time,

00:35:36.680 --> 00:35:40.240
go and use the
pricing calculator.

00:35:40.240 --> 00:35:45.670
So whether it's APIs or building
your own models on Cloud ML

00:35:45.670 --> 00:35:48.100
Engine, you want to
think where can I

00:35:48.100 --> 00:35:52.820
start using machine learning
to make my applications better,

00:35:52.820 --> 00:35:55.330
and there's tons and
tons of applications.

00:35:55.330 --> 00:36:00.370
I was at Next last week,
and as I listened and sat in

00:36:00.370 --> 00:36:02.800
on the talks about
machine learning,

00:36:02.800 --> 00:36:04.930
I kept thinking of
all of the software

00:36:04.930 --> 00:36:06.880
that I've developed
over the last five

00:36:06.880 --> 00:36:11.350
years and all the places that
I could use machine learning.

00:36:11.350 --> 00:36:15.590
So sentiment analysis on
comments, churn prediction,

00:36:15.590 --> 00:36:19.540
up-sell prediction,
conversion predictions--

00:36:19.540 --> 00:36:20.590
all tremendous things.

00:36:20.590 --> 00:36:22.630
So we had done a little
bit of data science

00:36:22.630 --> 00:36:24.310
at my last company,
where we determined

00:36:24.310 --> 00:36:32.540
that if a customer got to
one hour of calls every day

00:36:32.540 --> 00:36:35.060
for every box that they
had purchased, that they

00:36:35.060 --> 00:36:36.530
would be a customer forever.

00:36:36.530 --> 00:36:40.280
Their usage kept going
up, and they would renew.

00:36:40.280 --> 00:36:44.690
And we found that any customer
who didn't hit that milestone

00:36:44.690 --> 00:36:49.290
would actually churn, and it
was very high correlation,

00:36:49.290 --> 00:36:51.550
so it was fairly
good predictability.

00:36:51.550 --> 00:36:54.620
But with this, it would
have been a lot easier.

00:36:54.620 --> 00:36:59.030
That was a lot of manual
work in building processes

00:36:59.030 --> 00:37:00.920
on local resources.

00:37:00.920 --> 00:37:04.767
With this, we would be much
better off feeding as much data

00:37:04.767 --> 00:37:06.350
in with a bunch of
features and having

00:37:06.350 --> 00:37:09.110
it build the model for us.

00:37:09.110 --> 00:37:12.890
So after today's session, as
you begin to play with the APIs,

00:37:12.890 --> 00:37:16.130
or you begin to explore
building, training, deploying,

00:37:16.130 --> 00:37:18.500
and using your own
models, where do you

00:37:18.500 --> 00:37:21.200
look for additional information?

00:37:21.200 --> 00:37:23.480
As always, you
start with search.

00:37:23.480 --> 00:37:27.290
So if you type in Google
Cloud and the API name,

00:37:27.290 --> 00:37:30.260
then you're going to get
information about the Vision

00:37:30.260 --> 00:37:33.110
API or the Translation API.

00:37:33.110 --> 00:37:36.560
If you type in Google
Cloud ML and a topic, then

00:37:36.560 --> 00:37:40.370
you'll get information about the
machine learning capabilities

00:37:40.370 --> 00:37:41.940
with Cloud ML Engine.

00:37:41.940 --> 00:37:44.720
So for instance, one of
the things that Google does

00:37:44.720 --> 00:37:51.680
is they support the use of GPUs
for training your models using

00:37:51.680 --> 00:37:55.250
Cloud ML Engine, so if you
type in Google Cloud ML GPU,

00:37:55.250 --> 00:37:57.680
you'll get articles that
tell you how to do it,

00:37:57.680 --> 00:38:01.560
how much it costs,
stuff like that.

00:38:01.560 --> 00:38:04.890
Again, you have sort of
the standard organization

00:38:04.890 --> 00:38:08.250
of documentation
for Cloud ML Engine,

00:38:08.250 --> 00:38:09.720
so you've got the six quadrants.

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:12.510
You've got a Quick Start,
which that's super quick.

00:38:12.510 --> 00:38:14.970
That's just basically
installing TensorFlow

00:38:14.970 --> 00:38:17.970
and being able to do something
very, very, very basic.

00:38:17.970 --> 00:38:20.070
Then you have the how-to
guides and the tutorials,

00:38:20.070 --> 00:38:21.611
and this is where
you're going to get

00:38:21.611 --> 00:38:24.510
into that relatively
lengthy walkthrough

00:38:24.510 --> 00:38:27.060
and some other better examples.

00:38:27.060 --> 00:38:28.910
The documentation
for the APIs is

00:38:28.910 --> 00:38:31.410
a little different than this,
but again, if you just search,

00:38:31.410 --> 00:38:33.360
it'll take you directly
to the articles

00:38:33.360 --> 00:38:35.050
that you're looking for.

00:38:35.050 --> 00:38:36.750
So that's it for this session.

00:38:36.750 --> 00:38:38.319
Thanks so much for joining us.

00:38:38.319 --> 00:38:40.110
I hope you found it
interesting, and you're

00:38:40.110 --> 00:38:43.050
inspired to incorporate
machine learning capabilities

00:38:43.050 --> 00:38:44.970
into your own applications.

00:38:44.970 --> 00:38:47.640
We'll be sending you an email
with a link to a code lab,

00:38:47.640 --> 00:38:49.860
and you can use that to get
some hands-on experience

00:38:49.860 --> 00:38:51.300
with machine
learning capabilities

00:38:51.300 --> 00:38:52.890
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:38:52.890 --> 00:38:55.800
We'd love your feedback, so
feel free to provide feedback

00:38:55.800 --> 00:38:59.699
through the UI on your
screen and thanks very much.

